# Linear search
Q. What Is Linear Search?

Standard Definition

- Linear Search is a searching algorithm where we start from the first element of an array and check each element one by one until:
. the target element is found, or
. we reach the end of the array.

Pseudocode of Linear Search

start from index 0
for each element in the array
if element = = target
return index
if loop ends
return -1

Why pseudocode matters:

. It shows the logic clearly
. It is language independent
. It helps you think before writing code
Java Code Example: Linear Search

public class LinearSearchExample {
public static void main(String[] args) {

int[] numbers = {3, 8, 4, 12, 7, 15};
int target = 12;
boolean found = false;

for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
if (numbers[i] == target) {
System.out.println("Found " + target + " at index " + i);
found = true;
break;
}
    if (!found) {
       System.out.printIn(target + " is not in the array.");
    }
 }
}

